WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.812
A business orders a connection. Then waits thirty-five days for service.

00:00:04.972 --> 00:00:12.819
Turkcell, Türk Telekom and Vodafone are familiar names. This story follows a fictional operator, not those companies.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:20.661
One business order passes through customer records, the order desk, capacity checks, field teams and partners.

00:00:20.821 --> 00:00:24.201
Each step means passing the details to someone else.

00:00:24.961 --> 00:00:30.304
Staff type the same details again. Imagine one order number copied incorrectly.

00:00:30.464 --> 00:00:36.127
Someone must check the mistake, correct it and type it again. The customer is still waiting.

00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:44.683
Partners across the Balkans and Caucasus cannot exchange order details directly with the operator's systems.

00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:50.164
Staff must pass information along. More chasing. Less clarity about progress.

00:00:50.924 --> 00:00:55.756
The case study says thirty percent of orders get stuck and need more work.

00:00:55.916 --> 00:00:59.478
The same work comes back, while new orders keep arriving.

00:01:00.238 --> 00:01:06.424
Thirty-five days from order to working service. Five weeks before the customer can get started.

00:01:06.584 --> 00:01:11.329
That is the overall delay, not a measured wait for any individual team.

00:01:12.089 --> 00:01:18.178
The customer wants service quickly and clear progress updates. Instead, questions pile up.

00:01:18.338 --> 00:01:23.714
Delays put business relationships at risk. Waiting becomes a reason to leave.

00:01:24.474 --> 00:01:31.598
Demand for business connections and online services is growing faster than staff can handle by hand.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:39.001
Partners are choosing providers that make exchanging orders easier. Repeated work makes it harder to compete.

00:01:39.761 --> 00:01:45.819
Thirty-five days to start service. Thirty percent of orders need more work. Customers wait.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:50.706
Customer trust is at risk. Revenue waits. Competitors do not.
